Thus the eigenvalues of DŜ(0, t a ) are strictly less than one in modulus. We say that a point p0 has a basin B for f if for every
q ∈ B, the iterates f n (q) converge to p0 uniformly in a neighborhood of q. In general, p0 need not belong to B. It follows that
(0, t a ) ∈ E has a basin of attraction for Ŝ, which in fact contains a neighborhood of (0, t a ). Pushing down by π to a neighborhood
of (0, 0) ∈ C2 , we have:
Theorem 2.2. Let t a be as in Proposition 2.1. For sufficiently small r > 0, the set {(x, y) ∈ C2 : |x| < r, |t a − y/x| < r} is contained
in the basin of (0, 0) for S f . Thus for a multiple zero α of f , there is a conical neighborhood of α̂ in which the iterates of S f converge
to α̂ at the asymptotic rate O(|ψ0 (t a )|n ).

