Combining the cases a∞ < 0 and a∞ ≥ 0, we have:
Theorem 5.1. If p(t) = c(t − a)d , and deg(p) > deg(q), then δ = d t = d − 1. Otherwise, S f is of small topological degree, meaning
that the dynamical degree is larger than the topological degree.
    If δ > d t , there are invariant currents T ± with the properties S ∗ T + = δ T + and S∗ T − = δ T − (see [2]). In our argument
discussing the forward iterates of the exceptional curves, we in fact showed that the forward orbits of exceptional curves remain
at bounded distance from the indeterminacy locus. Thus by [4], it follows that there is an invariant measure µ = T + ∧ T − . A
number of the dynamical properties of T ± and µ are presented in [3]. It will be interesting to apply them to the Secant Method.

